4. Loop/branch: when `count*size > 4`, the code seeks and calls `io.read(buf.pData_, count*size)`. The same overflow can make the allocated buffer much smaller than the requested read length, producing heap corruption when enough stream data exists.
5. Branch: later XMP/ICC handling also uses raw `count`: `buf.pData_[count]=0` and `out.write(buf.pData_, count)`, which can exceed a wrapped, undersized allocation.
6. Loop/branch: recursive TIFF processing calls `printIFDStructure` once per count entry using file-controlled offsets with no maximum depth or visited-offset set, allowing self-referential IFDs and unbounded recursion.
7. Ordinary directory indexing uses a 32-byte buffer and 12-byte entries, but unchecked 32-bit count arithmetic and recursive offsets create memory-safety and denial-of-service paths.

